Instead of printing each star immediately, this version builds each row with `StringBuilder`. After the row is complete, it prints the row and starts a fresh builder for the next line.

A quick dry run for `n = 2` builds `"* * "` twice, giving a two by two square.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern1(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            StringBuilder currentRow = new StringBuilder();

            for (int column = 0; column < n; column++) {
                currentRow.append("* ");
            }

            System.out.println(currentRow);
        }
    }
}
```

Each row needs `O(n)` work and temporary space. Across all rows, the time complexity is `O(n^2)`, while the extra space is `O(n)`. The method also handles a single-row case, where it prints just one row of stars.

The same row is printed repeatedly, so there is no need to build every row separately. A string containing `n` stars is made once, then printed `n` times.

This keeps the printing logic small. The string uses a space after each star, which matches the usual pattern format.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern1(int n) {
        string row;
        for (int column = 0; column < n; column++) {
            row += "* ";
        }

        for (int line = 0; line < n; line++) {
            cout << row << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

The row takes `O(n)` space, while the output work takes `O(n^2)` time. Extra space is `O(n)`. When `n` is zero, the row is empty and no lines are printed.

A direct nested-loop version works well here. The outer loop controls the rows, and the inner loop prints `n` stars in every row.

For `n = 3`, the output has three rows, and each row contains three stars.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern1(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            for (int column = 0; column < n; column++) {
                System.out.print("* ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because one loop prints all `n * n` stars. Space complexity: `O(1)`. For `n = 0`, both loops are skipped and nothing is printed.

This version uses one recursive call for each row. `print_row` creates a complete row, prints it, and then moves to the next row.

A small dry run with `n = 2` is: print `* *`, call the function for row `1`, print `* *`, then stop when the row number reaches `2`.

```python
class Solution:
    def pattern1(self, n):
        def print_row(row_number):
            if row_number == n:
                return

            for column in range(n):
                print("*", end=" ")
            print()
            print_row(row_number + 1)

        print_row(0)
```

The nested loop still prints `n^2` stars, so the time complexity is `O(n^2)`. The recursive calls use `O(n)` stack space. An input of `n = 0` reaches the stopping condition immediately.

A square pattern has the same row every time. Python can create that row with string repetition, then a loop can print it once per line.

For example, when `n = 3`, `"* " * 3` becomes one row with three stars. The input is not changed.

```python
class Solution:
    def pattern1(self, n):
        row = "* " * n
        for _ in range(n):
            print(row)
```

Creating the row takes `O(n)` time and space. Printing it `n` times produces `O(n^2)` output, so the total time complexity is `O(n^2)`. The stored row uses `O(n)` extra space. Negative or zero `n` values result in no output because the range is empty.

